  "id": "Compound::DB01029 - Disease::DOID:9352",
  "verbalization": "Irbesartan, a member of the angiotensin II receptor antagonists class, is consistently linked to Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus through its inclusion in this class and its direct treatment effect. The evidence suggests that Irbesartan's therapeutic relationship with Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus is mediated through its pharmacological action on angiotensin II receptors, which is supported by multiple paths in the dataset. These paths highlight a shared ancestry with other angiotensin II receptor antagonists, such as Losartan and Valsartan, which in turn are associated with the disease. The lowest common ancestors of Irbesartan and its class members point to a common pathway involving biphenylyltetrazole and angiotensin II receptor antagonism, reinforcing the notion that Irbesartan's mechanism of action is central to its therapeutic effect on Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus. The consistent scores across the paths indicate a robust and reliable connection, suggesting that the relationship is well-supported by the available data.",
